 ### [Quadric Error Metric Tetrahedral Simplification](8981-quadric-error-metric-tetrahedral-simplification.md)
-Supports volumetric (tetrahedral) meshes and scalar field data, extending the error metric to 3D volume elements.
+Extends QEM to tetrahedral meshes for volumetric data, adding positional correction for stability.
 
 ### [Rapid Decimation for Direct Volume Rendering](15015-rapid-decimation-for-direct-volume-rendering.md)
-Converts grid cells into hybrid grids with many-sided cells by removing unimportant vertices, tailored for volume rendering pipelines.
+Uses data-value heuristics on hex/tet grids for volume rendering, rather than geometric QEM on triangles.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Dominated by sorting and kernel launches per iteration. With `MAX_ITERATIONS=2`, it is significantly faster than full greedy decimation [S2, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for vertices, faces, quadrics, edges, and adjacency structures. Thrust device vectors are used for parallel operations [S2, unofficial].
-- **Performance:** Optimized for GPU memory bandwidth and parallelism. Uses Thrust for sorting and scanning, and CUDA kernels for cost calculation and mesh updates [S2, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Fast, preserves sharp features, handles boundary preservation and topological validity checks on the device [S1][S2, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** The actual number of vertices may differ slightly from `target_vertex_count` due to topology constraints [S2, unofficial]. Limited iterations may not reach the exact target [S2, unofficial]. Numerical instability can cause spikes if not clamped [S2, unofficial].
-- **When to Use:** When rapid simplification of meshes (e.g., from analytic marching) is needed for rendering and texturing [S1].
-- **When Not to Use:** When exact vertex count is critical or maximum geometric fidelity is required over speed [S2, unofficial].
+- **Time Complexity:** Dominated by sorting edges ($O(E \log E)$) and adjacency building ($O(F)$) per iteration. With `MAX_ITERATIONS=2`, it is significantly faster than iterative CPU methods that run until convergence [S2, unofficial].
+- **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for vertices, faces, quadrics ($O(V)$), edges ($O(F)$), and adjacency structures ($O(F)$).
+- **Performance:** Optimized for GPU memory bandwidth and parallelism using Thrust and CUDA kernels [S2, unofficial]. Typical usage targets 10,000 vertices, but performance scales with mesh size [S2, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Fast, preserves sharp features via QEM, handles boundary preservation and topological validity checks on-device [S2, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:** The actual vertex count may differ slightly from the target due to topology constraints [S2, unofficial]. Limited iterations (2) may not reach the exact target count for complex meshes [S2, unofficial]. Input tensors must be contiguous [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Originating from the Quadric Edge Collapse Decimation (QECD) algorithm [S1], this method was adapted for GPU acceleration to handle the large polygon counts produced by analytic marching of implicit functions [S1]. It is implemented in the AnalyticMesh package and uses CUDA and Thrust for parallel processing [S2, unofficial]. It is used to simplify meshes for rendering and texturing [S1].
+The method is based on Quadric Edge Collapse Decimation (QECD), originally proposed by Garland and Heckbert [S1][S3, unofficial]. It was implemented on GPU using Thrust and CUDA kernels by Hwanhuh et al. in the `spcumc` repository [S2, unofficial]. It is used as a postprocessing step in the AnalyticMesh package to simplify meshes generated by analytic marching for efficient rendering and texturing [S1].
 
